  "def-pc-smooth-projective-curve"
 ],
 "proof": [
  {
   "text": "[thm-pc-picard-representable] of the Picard chapter – the Picard functor\nof a smooth projective curve over a separably closed field is\nrepresentable, with the full decomposition carried out there (*reference: Stacks Project, Picard Schemes of Curves, Proposition 44.6.6, [Tag 0B9Z](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B9Z)*); cf. Mumford, Abelian Varieties; Milne, Abelian Varieties, Ch. 12. We outline the\nclassical construction; the existence and property statements are\ntheorems of the sources above.",
   "uses": [
    "thm-pc-picard-representable"
   ],
   "elementary": false
  },
  {
   "text": "*(1) The Picard functor.* $\\operatorname{Pic}_{C/k}$ and\n$\\operatorname{Pic}^0_{C/k}\\subseteq\\operatorname{Pic}_{C/k}$ are\nas in [def-sc-picard-functor]; for $k$ algebraically closed, $C$ is a\nsmooth connected curve in the sense of [def-sc-pic0] by\n[lem-pc-curves-are-sc], and\n$\\operatorname{Pic}^0_{C/k}(k)=\\operatorname{Pic}^0(C)$ of\n[def-sc-pic0].",
   "uses": [
    "def-sc-picard-functor",
    "def-sc-pic0",
    "lem-pc-curves-are-sc"
   ],
   "elementary": false
  },
  {
   "text": "*(2) Representability.* The theorem of Grothendieck states\nthat $\\operatorname{Pic}_{C/k}$ (which is the functor of [def-pc-picard-functor] by [lem-pc-picard-functor-agrees-sc]) is represented by a group scheme\nlocally of finite type over $k$ ([thm-pc-picard-representable] of the Picard chapter; *reference: Stacks Project, Picard Schemes of Curves, Proposition 44.6.6, [Tag 0B9Z](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0B9Z)*). The\nsubfunctor $\\operatorname{Pic}^0_{C/k}$ is represented by an open\nand closed subgroup scheme ([prop-pd-picard-degree-decomposition],\nparts (2) and (3)).",
   "uses": [
    "def-pc-picard-functor",
    "lem-pc-picard-functor-agrees-sc",
    "thm-pc-picard-representable",
    "prop-pd-picard-degree-decomposition"
   ],
   "elementary": false
  },
  {
   "text": "*(3) Smoothness and the tangent space.* By\n[lem-sc-pic-tangent-space], the tangent space of\n$\\operatorname{Pic}_{C/k}$ at the identity is\n$H^1(C,\\mathcal O_C)$, of dimension $g$ ([def-pc-genus]);\nby [thm-sc-theorem-of-the-base],\n$\\operatorname{Pic}_{C/k}$ is a disjoint union of smooth\nvarieties of dimension $g$.",
   "uses": [
    "lem-sc-pic-tangent-space",
    "def-pc-genus",
    "thm-sc-theorem-of-the-base"
   ],
   "elementary": false
  },
  {
   "text": "*(4) Properness.* $\\operatorname{Pic}^0_{C/k}$ is proper by\n[thm-sc-theorem-of-the-base] (the ``theorem of the base'').",
   "uses": [
    "thm-sc-theorem-of-the-base"
   ],
   "elementary": false
  },
  {
   "text": "*(5) Abelian variety and base change.* By (2)–(4),\n$J_C:=\\operatorname{Pic}^0_{C/k}$ is a proper smooth connected\ngroup scheme, i.e. an abelian variety\n([def-sc-abelian-variety]), of dimension $g$. For\n$k$ algebraically closed, $J_C(k)=\\operatorname{Pic}^0(C)$\n([def-sc-picard-functor], [def-sc-pic0]) by\nrepresentability, and base change\ncompatibility $J_{C_{k'}}\\cong(J_C)_{k'}$ holds because the Picard\nfunctor commutes with base change. Finally, $J_C$ is projective\nby [lem-sc-abelian-variety-projective].\n\nThe hypothesis that $k$ is separably closed enters this proof exactly as in\nthe Picard chapter: it is what produces a $k$-rational point of $C$, from\nwhich the representability theorem [thm-pc-picard-representable] of that\nchapter deduces the fppf sheaf property of the naive Picard presheaf and hence\nrepresentability. For a curve over a general ground field the conclusion is\nnot asserted in this blueprint; see [rem-sc-jacobian-scope].",
   "uses": [
    "def-sc-abelian-variety",
    "def-sc-picard-functor",
    "def-sc-pic0",
    "lem-sc-abelian-variety-projective",
    "thm-pc-picard-representable"
   ],
   "elementary": false
  }
 ],
